4. Application Support / Incident Management Questions
21) What is your approach to incident triage?
Sample answer:
My approach starts with understanding business impact, urgency, affected users or plants, and whether the issue is isolated or systemic. Then I classify the incident by module/process area, identify whether it is a data, configuration, integration, authorization, or user-process issue, and prioritize accordingly. I ensure the right stakeholders are involved early, especially if production continuity is at risk. After immediate containment, I drive root cause analysis and preventive action.
22) How do you meet SLA targets in support operations?
Sample answer:
Meeting SLA targets requires a combination of structured triage, clear ownership, prioritization, and regular follow-up. I track incident queues, categorize issues by severity, and ensure aging tickets are proactively managed. For repeat issues, I focus on permanent fixes so the team is not overloaded. I also use dashboards and daily reviews to maintain visibility and accountability.
23) What do you do when a P1 incident is raised?
Sample answer:
For a P1 incident, I first establish the business impact and activate the response bridge with relevant teams. I focus on containment and restoring operations as quickly as possible, even if the root cause is not yet fully known. I keep stakeholders updated regularly, assign clear owners, document actions taken, and ensure a post-incident RCA is completed afterward. Communication and speed are both critical in P1 handling.
24) How do you perform root cause analysis?
Sample answer:
I use a structured method, often aligned with DMAIC thinking. First, I clearly define the issue and gather evidence such as logs, ticket history, user inputs, system behavior, timestamps, and affected transactions. Then I analyze patterns to identify the true source rather than the visible symptom. After implementing the fix, I validate results and document preventive actions so the incident does not recur.
25) How do you handle escalation prevention?
Sample answer:
Escalation prevention comes from proactive communication, realistic timelines, and visible progress. Even if a resolution takes time, stakeholders are more confident when they know what is being done, what the impact is, and when the next update will come. I also try to identify risks early and involve the right teams before an issue becomes critical.
26) How do you document issues and resolutions?
Sample answer:
I document the issue summary, business impact, root cause, affected components, actions taken, resolution, and preventive recommendations. Good documentation helps future troubleshooting, improves knowledge sharing, supports audits, and reduces dependency on individual team members.
